Depth as a 3D source (ORDER 151, step R part 2): a video with its depth map (2D plus depth) is decoded like any other packing and handed to the compositor as full side by side. QVideoFrameFormat gains the depth layouts of the edition's depth marks (StereoMode DepthSideBySide, DepthTopBottom, DepthGraphics, DepthBackground, DepthSeparate), StereoFlag::DepthFirst (0x8: Multiview 0x2 and BaseViewRight 0x4 of the MV-HEVC patch keep their values) and stereoDepthMark(). The FFmpeg backend reads the STEREO3D_LAYOUT and STEREO3D_DEPTH tags of packed videos, the depth auxiliary layer of H.265 (AuxId 2) and the ISO/IEC 23002-3 depth streams, and pairs each picture with its depth map. VideoOutput draws the two views with libstereo-dibr, one picture per eye at the size the video holds, no resize before the compositor, with every choice of libstereo-dibr as a property (stereoDepth, stereoDepthView, Warp, Fill, Radius, Smooth, Range, Zero, Shift, Meaning, Near, Far, Mark); the depth layouts are values of stereoInput.

A depth layer of H.265 is decoded in software, which no hardware decoder outputs, and so is every stream whose hardware frames would lose bits or that has no hardware format of the routed device: the log says so in each case, never silently.

A converted full range frame stays full range and keeps its color and side data: a depth map squeezed into the video range drew the views with 85% of their parallax. The format callback takes the software format when no hardware format is chosen: a stream the bit depth check turned away from NVDEC was decoded there after all.

The eye chosen on a paused frame is shown: the node kept the source rectangle of the eye shown before.

QAudioDecoder with the SoX resampler, the edition's default: a frame that gives no samples yet no longer stalls the decoding, and the samples the resampler holds back come out at the end; tst_qaudiodecoderbackend failed with +stereo3d13 and passes again.

debian/control: Build-Depends libstereo-dibr-dev (>= 1.9.0); libqt6multimedia6 depends on libavutil61 (>= 10:9.0.2-dmo1+stereo3d5~), which has av_multiview_real_to_double and the depth side data the FFmpeg backend reads (the link gate found the symbol undefined against Debian's libavutil).

A depth video recorded sideways or upside down is drawn the right way up: the views of a picture with its depth map are drawn from the picture and the depth map turned by the frame's rotation and mirroring (its surface's and its own), so that the parallax runs along the width of the picture as it is shown, and the drawn frame carries no transformation to apply; the compositor layer shows a rotated depth video too, where it had been refused one. The compositor path of a depth video is proved in the isolated Stereo KWin (tst_qquickvideocroplayer, depthNativeSubmission): full side by side, the left eye first, each eye at the picture's size, every sample the picture's own.

A depth video is drawn the best way that keeps its frame rate, on the GPU (ORDER 411, group 2e; Daniel, 2026-11-05: "we choose the best default and left options to the user"; the players on the measured best GPU). VideoOutput draws the views with libstereo-dibr's GLSL passes in an OpenGL context of its own, on the GPU the process draws with: the planes of 8 bit planar and semi-planar YUV frames are uploaded as they are and turned into RGBA by a pass here (BT.601, BT.709 and BT.2020, limited or full range), the frames of hardware decoders and of other formats arrive as RGBA images, and the full side by side frame is read back for the player. The C code on the processor draws what the GPU does not read (10 bit and 16 bit video, formats with their own byte order) and what no OpenGL context can be made for, and the log of qt.multimedia.video.depth says so, with the reason, whenever it changes: never silently. The frames of a hardware decoder are not imported into the context without a copy: they are read back by qImageFromVideoFrame() as before and uploaded.

The way (the warping and the gap filling) is automatic until the program picks one: the first way of libstereo-dibr's ladder (layered with depth-aided fill, layered with the pyramid fill, forward with the pyramid fill, forward with background fill) whose slowest frames fit four fifths of the frame's time on the GPU (or processor) that draws, timed once for each GPU and size and kept in the person's cache, never in the media file. New VideoOutput properties: stereoDepthAutomatic (setting stereoDepthWarp or stereoDepthFill picks the way and turns it off, and the pick holds for the video, the slowest way included), stereoDepthGpu (false draws on the processor), stereoDepthChoice (what was chosen and why: the way, whether it keeps up, the best way that does not, where it draws), and the StereoDepthFillPyramid fill. The defaults of the depth properties are libstereo-dibr's, read from stereo_dibr_options_default(): the library has one default.

The depth drawing watches the GPU (ORDER 411, step 2f): the context asks for reset notification (KHR_robustness) and the library's reset status ends the GPU path for the video when the GPU was reset, with the C code drawing, the log saying so and stereoDepthChoice carrying gpuLost; the ladder is timed from the cheapest way up for the video's frame rate, stops at the first way that misses it, paces its passes in bands of rows, and does not run a way that needs more than the GPU has free (skippedMs is then -2; the cheapest way is always run).

Deep colour on the GPU path (ORDER 411, step 2g; Daniel, 2026-10-07: video "at the source's depth (10/12/16 bits, hardware decode only where it keeps depth) ... surfaces at 10 bits or half float"): P010, P016, YUV420P10, YUV422P16 and YUV444P16 frames, every high bit depth format the FFmpeg backend hands Qt, go to the passes as the words they are (R16 and RG16 textures, the code of each format read by the conversion pass: P010 and the 16 bit formats in the high bits, YUV420P10 in the low bits), the depth samples are R16UI integers read through the table of the C code, the passes' colour textures are half float up to 10 bits and float above, and the views leave in the source's pixel format and depth: the full side by side frame is never cut to 8 bits. The C code draws deep content only when the person asks for the processor (and the log says so), or on an OpenGL ES without GL_EXT_texture_norm16. A frame the decoder keeps on a GPU (VA-API) is mapped into the drawing context by the decoder's texture converter (QHwVideoBuffer::mapTextures through a QRhi sharing the context) and read where it is, with no copy to system memory; a converter that refuses it, a turned frame, or QT_MM_DEPTH_COPY_FRAMES copies it, counted and said in the log with the reason. New VideoOutput property stereoDepthGpuMemory (what the drawing GPU has free, for the controls to say beside a way that would not fit).

The drawn views go to the video sink as GPU textures, and the decoder's frames reach the passes without a copy on NVIDIA and on AMD (ORDER 411, step 2h; Daniel, 2026-10-23: "every player takes the efficient path to the screen by default: which GPU renders and decodes, and how frames reach the GPU that drives the display"). When the video sink draws with OpenGL, the drawing context shares its textures and the full side by side views leave it as a QHwVideoBuffer of GL textures (RGBA8 for 8 bit content, the source's own R16 and RG16 planes for 10 to 16 bit content), which the scene graph takes through textureHandle() as it takes the textures of QOpenGLVideoBuffer: no read back to system memory. A consumer that maps the frame gets a read back, counted: in the compositor's stereo layer (the declared full side by side Stereo KWin takes) the layer's presenter maps the frame, converts it to the buffer format of the output and copies it into shared memory buffers on a thread of its own, so that path still reads the views back, and stereoDepthRoute says so (layer true, views readBack); a zero copy hand-over to the compositor (the views as DMA-BUF buffers) is the next step. The frames of textures look at the drawer without keeping it alive: it goes with the video output, before the decoder's own state that its converters use (a CUDA converter pushes the decoder's CUDA context when it is let go). Where the sink draws with another API, shares no context, or QT_MM_DEPTH_COPY_VIEWS is set, the views are read back and the log says why. New VideoOutput property stereoDepthRoute (how the decoder's frames travel: imported, copied and why, memory; how the views travel: texture, readBack and why, memory) and method stereoDepthReadBacks().

NVDEC frames are mapped without Qt's read back: the CUDA hw-texture converter of Qt's dev branch (FFmpeg: Add CUDA hw-texture converter for NVDEC decode on Linux, Tim Blechmann, c57f4af5ab, 2026-09-16, with the vendored ffnvcodec header 2506a582c8 and the Vulkan helpers of fad9a642f0, the Vulkan part only) is carried; the 6.11.2 texture converter makes its backend through the converter's factory. It copies the planes of NVDEC's frames from device memory into a GL texture of the drawing context (a GPU to GPU copy, not a read back to system memory).

The planes of an imported frame are made readable by the passes and checked: Qt's VA-API converter leaves the textures it makes of the decoder's DMA-BUFs with a minification filter that needs mipmaps, which reads as zeros in a pass that reads level 0 by texel; the filters are set to nearest before the passes read them (found on the A10, where the first import drew a green picture), and the planes of the first frames of a stream, and of every sixtieth, are sampled at the middle of the picture: luma and chroma all zero is no picture, and the import is refused, the frames are copied from then on and the log says why.

The compositor's stereo layer shows the drawn views without a read back and without a copy of ours (ORDER 411, step 2i; Daniel, 2026-10-23: "which GPU renders and decodes, and how frames reach the GPU that drives the display"). For the drawn views of a depth video the layer makes an EGL context that shares the drawing context's textures and a window surface for its wl_surface (wl_egl_window: the driver exchanges the buffers with the compositor, as DMA-BUF where both take them, with their modifiers, release and synchronisation) and draws the two eyes with one pass: the crop, the eye order and the surface's format, at the depth of the views (RGBA8 into an 8 bit surface; 10 to 16 bit content into a 10 bit or a half float surface, never a surface of less depth). Where the driver has no surface of that depth, or the frame is not drawn views, or QT_MM_STEREO_LAYER_SHM=1, the presenter converts and copies the frame into wl_shm buffers as before, counted and said with the reason (stereoDepthRoute: layer, layerDmabuf, views, viewsWhy; stereoDepthReadBacks(): layerGpuFrames, layerCopiedFrames). QT_MM_STEREO_LAYER_DUMP=dir dumps the first frames of either path for the proofs.

The colour matrix of a depth video that does not say is the one Qt's own conversion assumes (HD BT.709, SD BT.601), and the drawn frame says it: a deep depth video of an untagged HD stream was drawn and encoded with BT.601 and decoded with BT.709 by the scene graph and the layer's presenter.

NVDEC's 12 bit frames (HEVC Main 12 and VP9 profile 2 at 12 bits, AV_PIX_FMT_P012) are mapped by the CUDA converter like P010 and P016, and its fully planar 4:4:4 frames (YUV444P, YUV444P16 and, with FFmpeg 8 and later, YUV444P10MSB and YUV444P12MSB) are imported as three planes by the converter and the passes, not copied (ORDER 411, step 2j).

12 to 16 bit video reaches the compositor at 16 bits (ORDER 411, step 2j; Daniel, 2026-10-06: "we go up to 16, remember that", never capped at 12). Content above 10 bits, 12 bit included (half float holds about 11 significant bits), is drawn at 32 bit floating point and goes to the compositor in a 16 bit unsigned normalised surface (StereoSurface16Bit, the choice StereoSurfaceBySource makes above 10 bits). The layer takes, in this order: a window surface of the driver in that format (Mesa lists 16.16.16.16); buffers the layer allocates itself where the driver has none (NVIDIA lists 16 bit only as half float): GBM on the render node of the EGL device, each imported as an EGLImage, drawn into by the same pass and given to the compositor through zwp_linux_dmabuf_v1 with a format and a modifier that both the driver (for rendering) and the compositor's feedback list; wl_shm XBGR16161616, copied by the presenter, counted and said. 10 bit content takes ABGR2101010 or XRGB2101010 from the compositor's list before a half float window surface. QT_MM_STEREO_LAYER_DMABUF=1 draws every depth into the layer's own buffers and 0 never does. The depth route says what was observed (layerDmabuf: frames committed with the layer's own DMA-BUF buffers; layerDmabufOffered: what the compositor offers; layerPath, layerSurfaceFormat, layerBuffer).

A depth video no longer clears the stereo presenter at every frame: the output compared the format and the packing of the stored input with those of the drawn views, which differ at every frame, and the presenter's conversions were dropped as out of date (on the A10 the wl_shm control delivered no buffer in 9 seconds).

debian/control: Build-Depends libstereo-dibr-dev (>= 1.17.1).

debian/control: qml6-module-qtmultimedia Depends libgbm1 and libwayland-egl1, which the stereo layer loads when it draws the drawn views (EGL window surfaces, and GBM buffers where the driver has no window surface of the depth).
